From d29564184fda8c2a17727fd654bc833b4d999bcf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 13:31:56 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 02/10] CUDA: group same-activation quantized matvecs into one
 launch

Port of the R9700/HIP track patch 0004 ("CUDA/HIP: group same-activation
quantized matvecs into one launch", Claude Fable 5) to the GB10 CUDA
track. Up to 4 plain single-column mul_mat_vec_q ops over the SAME src1
run as one kernel whose grid concatenates their row ranges; a block routes
itself to its segment (weight pointer, dst pointer, local row index - all
integer work) and then performs exactly the per-row thread geometry,
k-loop and reduction of the individual launch it replaces. Group detection
and the hoist-legality rules are unchanged from the HIP patch.

Two CUDA-specific changes:

1. No idle-warp trim. The HIP series trims warps that would get no
   k-blocks (its patches 0001/0002) and the grouped launch reproduces that
   trimmed block shape. That series is deliberately NOT part of this CUDA
   track: on NVIDIA the launch geometry it targets does not occur, because
   the upstream small_k path - which widens rows_per_cuda_block to nwarps
   instead of leaving warps idle - is enabled here and disabled on RDNA
   (should_use_small_k in mul_mat_vec_q_switch_ncols_dst turns itself off
   for GGML_CUDA_CC_IS_RDNA). Whenever the trim condition
   blocks_per_row_x <= blocks_per_iter is strictly satisfied on this
   device, small_k has already fired; at equality the trim is a no-op.
   The grouped launch therefore uses the stock nwarps and the kernel drops
   the trimmed-warp shared-memory prefill, which is dead code without it.

2. Launch-shape guard on grouping eligibility. Because small_k IS live on
   NVIDIA, an individual mul_mat_vec_q here may take the
   rows_per_cuda_block == nwarps shape, whose per-row thread geometry
   differs from the rows_per_cuda_block == 1 shape the grouped kernel
   reproduces. ggml_cuda_mmvq_can_group now asks
   ggml_cuda_mmvq_group_shape_matches(type, ne00), which mirrors the base
   condition of should_use_small_k for ncols_dst == 1 and also re-checks
   calc_rows_per_block == 1. The type exclusion lists in should_use_small_k
   can only turn small_k off, so testing the base condition alone is
   conservative: it may decline to group an op that would not in fact use
   small_k, never the reverse. For Laguna-XS-2.1-Q4_K_M on sm_121 the
   attention projections (Q4_K, K=2048: blocks_per_row_x 8, nwarps 4,
   blocks_per_iter_1warp 2) keep the non-small_k shape and group; the
   short-K expert projections take small_k and are left alone.

GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_MMVQ_GROUP=1 disables the optimization;
GGML_MMVQ_GROUP_STATS=1 logs per-pass group counts.

The grouped path also shares one q8_1 quantization through the per-graph
cache, so its insert() uses the slab allocator from the previous patch and
falls back to a scoped allocation on a slab-full miss.

Verified on DGX Spark GB10 (sm_121, CUDA 13.0): greedy text and full float
top-5 logprob hashes match the stock build on 3 prompts.

On GB10 this lever turns out to be a loss and the next patch in this series
disables it by default; see that patch for the measurements.
